A null environ, and realpath's corner cases. glibc's clearenv(3) names
`environ = NULL` as the other way to clear the environment, and glibc
and musl take a null environ as an empty one; mlibc's getenv, setenv,
unsetenv and putenv read through it. A string without an equals sign
in the environment is no variable, and is passed over in silence, as in
glibc and musl: mlibc wrote each to the kernel's log on every lookup, so
a crafted environment could fill the log. realpath with a null buffer
used its allocation without checking it, and realpath(NULL, buf) is
EINVAL, as in glibc, not EFAULT. Meant for upstream.

--- a/options/ansi/generic/environment.cpp
+++ b/options/ansi/generic/environment.cpp
